# Guard wiring: two call sites, one argument, and the drift neither of them could see (2026-10-04)

`scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py` used to answer two questions: does every guard
under `scripts/ci/` get run by something, and does the local Docker entry point run
the guards CI runs. Both were reachability questions. Neither looked at what the two
call sites *ask* of a guard once they agree to run it, which is the half where the
2026-10-04 budget incident actually lives: `platform-gated-tests.py` is held to four
budgets, the numbers are written once in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` and once in
`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`, and a lowering reached one of the two files. CI blocked
at `--max-unreviewed 1106` while the leg developers run locally still accepted 1108.
Every check in the repository was green while that was true.

The rule added here: a flag one call site passes and the other never mentions stays a
choice -- `--require` is deliberately Windows-leg-only, and demanding it locally would
ask the container for something it cannot do. A flag both pass has to carry the same
values in both.

== 0. Where the incident had already left a scar

The same drift was fixed earlier the same day from the other direction.
`scripts/ci/test-platform-gated-tests.py` had re-typed the budgets into its own
constants to check the ledger against them, so when the call sites moved to 1106 and
441 the self-test went red on `1106 != 1108` and `441 != 443`. That fix removed the
third copy of the numbers and derived them from the wiring instead:

    docs/verification/platform-gated-tests-2026-10-03.log   section `== 11.`

Deriving them means the self-test can no longer disagree with the call sites, which is
correct, and also means nothing was left comparing the two call sites to each other.
The self-test now reports what both roots ask for; it has no position on whether they
should agree. That gap is what this file is about.

== 1. What the rule compares on this repository

The reader is `flags_by_gate()` in `scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py`. It walks the
same roots the reachability rule walks -- `.github/workflows/*.yml` and
`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` -- and builds, per root, a map of guard to flag to the
set of values that root passes. Dumped against the shipped tree:

