Twelve pairs, of which the four budgets are the ones that were observed to drift.
`release.yml` runs no guard with an argument, so it contributes nothing to compare.
The two `--require` rows appear on the CI side only and are therefore not compared,
which is the deliberate asymmetry the rule has to preserve.

== 2. The drift, replayed against the tree

`ci.yml` was edited to `--max-unreviewed 1107`, the local entry point left at 1106,
and three verdicts collected. The scratch copy of the checker is `HEAD`'s version, so
the first verdict is what the repository does today:

$ grep -n 'max-unreviewed' .github/workflows/ci.yml scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
    .github/workflows/ci.yml:722:            --max-unreviewed 1107 --max-blind-windows 74 --max-blind-macos 11 \
    scripts/verify-in-docker.sh:166:  "platform-gated tests: python3 scripts/ci/test-platform-gated-tests.py && python3 scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py --quiet --check-baseline scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv --max-unreviewed 1106 --max-blind-windows 74 --max-blind-macos 11 --max-assumption-free 441"
    [exit 0]

-- the checker as it stood at HEAD, i.e. before this rule existed:

$ python3 /tmp/grok-goal-ec37f68d1619/implementer/check-guard-wiring.head.py --root /home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code
    check-guard-wiring: OK (50 files in scripts/ci/, 49 reachable, 45 run by scripts/verify-in-docker.sh, 4 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)
    [exit 0]

-- the same tree against the checker with the agreement rule:

$ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
      platform-gated-tests.py --max-unreviewed is passed as '1107' by .github/workflows/ci.yml but '1106' by scripts/verify-in-docker.sh; a budget only has to be raised once, so both call sites have to move together
    check-guard-wiring: 1 problem(s)
    [exit 1]

-- and the platform-gated gate itself, which is what would have had to disagree:

$ python3 scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py --quiet --check-baseline scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv --max-unreviewed 1107 --max-blind-windows 74 --max-blind-macos 11 --max-assumption-free 441
    [exit 0]

The gate that owns the budget cannot report this at all: 1107 is a cap it is willing
to enforce. It is the pair that is wrong, and pairs are not visible from inside one
call site. `ci.yml` was restored byte-identically after the replay and its mtime
bumped.

== 3. Four attribution decisions, each of which was wrong in a first draft

Backslash continuations. The budgets in `ci.yml` do not sit on the line that names the
guard; they are on four continuation lines below it. Read line by line, the workflow
appears to pass no budget at all, and a flag present on one side only is not compared,
so the rule would have gone quiet on the exact case it exists for. `command_units()`
joins continuations before anything is matched.

Shell connectives. `verify-in-docker.sh` keeps a whole gate on one array line, and
several gates are `test-x.py --flag && x.py --budget N`. Splitting only at guard names
attributes the budget to the test file in front of it, which changes which pair is
being compared -- and in the first draft it also invented a disagreement on `--require`
whose second value was the string `&&`. Each command unit is cut at `&&`, `||`, `;` and
`|` first, and flags are attributed to the invocation ahead of them.

Comments. Both roots carry old numbers in prose explaining how a budget got here. If
those counted, the checker would report a disagreement between two sentences, and a
noisy gate is a gate that gets disabled. `command_units()` reuses `executable_lines()`,
which already strips YAML and shell prose, so the same rule that keeps docstrings from
counting as invocations keeps comments from counting as arguments.

Quoting. A gates-array element ends in `"`, so the last value on the line arrives as
`scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv"`. Values and flag names are stripped of shell
quoting before comparison, or every baseline path would disagree with itself.

One more decision was forced by a fixture that failed rather than by reasoning. The
comparison is the local entry point against *each* workflow, never workflow against
workflow: comparing all roots pairwise would make a second leg that legitimately wants
a different value impossible to add, and comparing only `ci.yml` by name would let a
second leg drift freely. Two workflows that both run a mirrored guard are therefore
each compared with the entry point.

== 4. Fixtures

`scripts/ci/test-check-guard-wiring.py`, class `FlagAgreementTests`, eight cases plus
one live case in `RealRepositoryTests`. Each pins one of the decisions above:

    test_the_same_value_at_both_call_sites_is_accepted
    test_a_value_raised_at_one_call_site_only_is_named
    test_a_flag_only_one_call_site_passes_is_a_choice
    test_a_budget_written_on_a_continued_line_still_counts
    test_a_flag_after_a_shell_connective_belongs_to_the_next_command
    test_a_baseline_only_one_call_site_reads_is_named
    test_a_commented_out_number_is_not_a_call_site
    test_a_second_workflow_has_to_agree_with_the_entry_point_too
    test_the_real_repo_actually_compares_the_four_budgets

The last one is the non-vacuity case, and it reads this repository rather than a
fixture. If the reader stopped matching the real call sites, all eight synthetic trees
could still pass while the four budgets went unwatched, so it asserts the four
platform-gated budgets really appear in the compared set, and that `--require` really
does not. It does not restate any number: after the lesson in section 0, a test that
re-typed `1106` would be the next thing to go stale.

$ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-guard-wiring.py -v
    ...
    Ran 25 tests in 1.756s

    OK
    [exit 0]

Seventeen fixtures before, twenty-five now.

