The 2.869s run is a separate invocation from the 1.756s `-v` run quoted in section 4;
both are reported as they came out. The replay in section 2 and the mutations in
section 5 each ended with `cmp` reporting the file identical to the copy taken before
the edit, and the working tree afterwards held only the files this batch changes:

$ git status --porcelain
     M CHANGELOG.md
     M TODO.md
     M docs/ci-test-debt.md
     M scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
     M scripts/ci/test-check-guard-wiring.py
    ?? docs/verification/guard-wiring-flag-agreement-2026-10-04.log

== 7. What is still open after this

The rule compares values, so it catches "one file was edited" and "one file was edited
differently". It cannot catch a budget that is right at both call sites and wrong in
principle -- a number nobody re-measured, or one that was lowered against a ledger that
had itself gone stale. That end is covered elsewhere: `scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py`
fails when the measured debt no longer matches, and its self-test now asserts that each
cap sits exactly one above the measured figure, which is what caught the drift in
`platform-gated-tests-2026-10-03.log` section 11.

What is not covered anywhere: a guard that two call sites both run with *no* argument
today, and later start passing different ones, is caught the moment the flag appears at
both -- but a flag that is added to only one side stays a choice the rule cannot judge.
That is the intended limit. A gate that needs different things on Linux and Windows is
a real thing in this repository, and a checker that forbade it would be worked around
rather than obeyed.
